WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.734
-Our next guest is
an Emmy-winning writer,

00:00:02.734 --> 00:00:04.567
director, and producer.
His latest movie,

00:00:04.567 --> 00:00:06.901
"Please Don't Destroy:
The Treasure of Foggy Mountain,"

00:00:06.901 --> 00:00:09.300
is streaming exclusively
on Peacock November 17th.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:10.634
Let's take a look.

00:00:10.634 --> 00:00:12.634
-I think I know where the
treasure of Foggy Mountain is,

00:00:12.634 --> 00:00:14.300
and I think
we should go get it.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:15.968
-The treasure of Foggy Mountain?

00:00:15.968 --> 00:00:17.934
Like, from when we were kids?

00:00:17.934 --> 00:00:20.100
-It all started
when I saw a TikTok

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:21.501
about this guy --

00:00:21.501 --> 00:00:23.667
Deetch Nordwind.

00:00:23.667 --> 00:00:25.667
-Whoa!

00:00:25.667 --> 00:00:27.601
You have like 40 tabs open.

00:00:27.601 --> 00:00:28.934
What are you doing, man?

00:00:28.934 --> 00:00:30.901
-Well, that's how much
research I've been doing.

00:00:30.901 --> 00:00:33.367
-Well, a lot of these are clips
from "Jennifer's Body."

00:00:33.367 --> 00:00:35.167
Was that part of the research?
-Um...

00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:36.767
-You could just rent the movie
at that point.

00:00:36.767 --> 00:00:38.267
-Yeah.
-Enough.

00:00:38.267 --> 00:00:39.334
-Please welcome
back to the show

00:00:39.334 --> 00:00:41.100
our friend Judd Apatow,
everybody!

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.167
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:43.167 --> 00:00:52.534
♪♪

00:00:52.534 --> 00:00:54.834
Welcome back!
-Yes!

00:00:54.834 --> 00:00:56.400
-I want to get to the movie,

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:57.901
but I also, I want
to start talking

00:00:57.901 --> 00:00:59.901
about your very talented
daughter, Maude.

00:00:59.901 --> 00:01:01.167
-Yes.

00:01:01.167 --> 00:01:03.567
-Who was recently doing
"Cabaret" in London.

00:01:03.567 --> 00:01:05.567
-That's right.
-Onstage in London.

00:01:05.567 --> 00:01:06.734
-Look at that.
-That's thrilling.

00:01:06.734 --> 00:01:08.400
That must be thrilling
as a father.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.234
-Oh, my God, it's so exciting.
I went 12 times.

00:01:10.234 --> 00:01:11.834
-Did you really go 12 times?
-I know.

00:01:11.834 --> 00:01:14.100
The cast sometimes
would look at me out there

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.300
like, "Again?"
-Yeah.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:19.300
So, tell me what it's like
as a father. Is it just...

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:22.300
Does it just fly by when you get
to watch a child onstage?

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:23.534
I mean, seeing it 12 times,

00:01:23.534 --> 00:01:25.701
does it just feel amazing
every time?

00:01:25.701 --> 00:01:28.968
-Uh, I'm so nervous
the first three or four times.

00:01:28.968 --> 00:01:30.901
-Yeah.
-And then I can enjoy it.

00:01:30.901 --> 00:01:32.734
And then by the end of the run,

00:01:32.734 --> 00:01:35.734
I feel like it's some
major accomplishment of mine.

00:01:35.734 --> 00:01:37.234
-Yes.
-You know?

00:01:37.234 --> 00:01:39.434
So it's, like, really fun
'cause I don't have to do it,

00:01:39.434 --> 00:01:41.200
but when it's over,
I'm like, "We did it."

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.534
[ Laughter ]

00:01:43.534 --> 00:01:45.534
-Now, one of the reasons
you're here promoting the movie

00:01:45.534 --> 00:01:47.167
is because of the SAG strike.

00:01:47.167 --> 00:01:48.501
-There is a strike.
I don't know if you know.

00:01:48.501 --> 00:01:50.334
-There is a strike.
-It's happening right now.

00:01:50.334 --> 00:01:52.000
-Yes, and yes.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:53.567
-It's a big strike.
-It's a big strike.

00:01:53.567 --> 00:01:55.934
And because of that,
the guys in the movie,

00:01:55.934 --> 00:01:58.200
because they're SAG actors,
cannot promote the film.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:01.000
You are a producer of the film,
so you can.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:03.501
But because you can,
and only you can...

00:02:03.501 --> 00:02:06.667
-I'm the only person
affiliated with the movie

00:02:06.667 --> 00:02:08.400
who's allowed to talk
about the movie.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.334
-Yeah.
-Everyone else has to pretend

00:02:10.334 --> 00:02:12.167
it does not exist.
-Yeah.

00:02:12.167 --> 00:02:13.501
And in fact, the three,

00:02:13.501 --> 00:02:15.267
the Please Don't Destroy guys
will be here tomorrow,

00:02:15.267 --> 00:02:16.767
and we're not gonna be able
to talk about it.

00:02:16.767 --> 00:02:18.534
-You'd better not!
-We won't.

00:02:18.534 --> 00:02:20.167
-I don't want you to mention.

00:02:20.167 --> 00:02:22.167
-It'll be weird 'cause
I'm talking to you about it now

00:02:22.167 --> 00:02:23.400
and they're gonna be here
tomorrow,

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:24.434
and they're gonna be like,
"Why is he not even

00:02:24.434 --> 00:02:25.934
bringing it up?"
-Yeah.

00:02:25.934 --> 00:02:28.267
-But because you're the only one
who can talk about it,

00:02:28.267 --> 00:02:30.367
it has affected things like
the press at the premiere.

00:02:30.367 --> 00:02:33.200
-Yeah, that's who showed up.
-Like...

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:35.834
-Just one guy who looks like
Evan Goldberg showed up.

00:02:35.834 --> 00:02:37.167
That's it.

00:02:37.167 --> 00:02:40.367
I mean, literally,
that's the entire press line.

00:02:40.367 --> 00:02:42.434
And that's with me
standing there going,

00:02:42.434 --> 00:02:44.100
"Please take my picture."

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:46.067
[ Laughter ]

00:02:46.067 --> 00:02:48.067
-So, this is
one of the cool things.

00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:50.534
The Please Don't Destroy guys,
who are wonderful --

00:02:50.534 --> 00:02:53.067
two of them, John Higgins
and Martin Herlihy,

00:02:53.067 --> 00:02:54.400
you know their dads.
-That's right.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:55.534
-You've known their dads
forever.

00:02:55.534 --> 00:02:57.400
-Yes.
John's dad is Steve Higgins,

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:58.901
the great producer
from "Saturday Night Live,"

00:02:58.901 --> 00:03:00.367
from "The Tonight Show."

00:03:00.367 --> 00:03:02.467
And Martin's dad is Tim Herlihy,

00:03:02.467 --> 00:03:05.267
who wrote "The Wedding Singer"
and "Happy Gilmore."

00:03:05.267 --> 00:03:07.667
-A famous SNL writer, as well.
-So it's very funny,

00:03:07.667 --> 00:03:09.434
like, working with their kids

00:03:09.434 --> 00:03:11.133
because they're young.
-Yeah.

00:03:11.133 --> 00:03:13.300
-I am no longer young.
-Yeah.

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:14.801
-And we would be sitting
in the woods

00:03:14.801 --> 00:03:17.701
having these conversations,
and I would have flashbacks

00:03:17.701 --> 00:03:20.734
of having the same conversations
with Adam Sandler and Tim,

00:03:20.734 --> 00:03:25.567
like, "Hm, I've had
this boner conversation before.

00:03:25.567 --> 00:03:27.734
I've debated this joke before."

00:03:27.734 --> 00:03:30.634
-No, I will say,
I would not want to ever talk

00:03:30.634 --> 00:03:33.801
to one of my dad's friends
about boners.

00:03:33.801 --> 00:03:35.634
But if I had to,

00:03:35.634 --> 00:03:37.300
I would think, like, Judd Apatow
would be the one.

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:38.701
Do they, like...?
-I would think that.

00:03:38.701 --> 00:03:40.067
-Yeah.
-I would think that too.

00:03:40.067 --> 00:03:42.400
But no matter what happens,
you're the creepy old guy.

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:43.801
-Yeah.
-You know? 'Cause, like...

00:03:43.801 --> 00:03:45.534
-I also want to note, I know
that you filmed it in the woods,

00:03:45.534 --> 00:03:47.033
but it was weird
that you started that by,

00:03:47.033 --> 00:03:48.901
like, "We'd be in the woods
talking about it."

00:03:48.901 --> 00:03:50.100
That didn't help.
-Well, no, no, no.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:51.400
I would lure them
into the woods.

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:52.567
-You would lure them.

00:03:52.567 --> 00:03:54.000
[ Laughter ]

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.567
I do want to talk about

00:03:55.567 --> 00:03:57.400
maybe the fact
that you were having flashbacks.

00:03:57.400 --> 00:03:58.734
These are some
of your early days.

00:03:58.734 --> 00:04:00.300
-Well, this is when
I was their age.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:02.400
-So, this is crazy.
This is when you were their age.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.167
These are some of the people
you were hanging out with.

00:04:04.167 --> 00:04:05.734
-Yeah. That's right.
-So, that's you.

00:04:05.734 --> 00:04:07.234
And what do you got there?

00:04:07.234 --> 00:04:10.234
-That's Shandling
and Springsteen at the Grammys.

00:04:10.234 --> 00:04:11.334
-Wow.
-That's at the Grammys.

00:04:11.334 --> 00:04:12.968
-And you were writing
at the Grammys?

00:04:12.968 --> 00:04:15.634
-That's my first job ever,
writing the Grammys.

00:04:15.634 --> 00:04:18.501
Obviously, I thought
I was kind of a cool

00:04:18.501 --> 00:04:21.167
Sylvester Stallone type
at the time.

00:04:21.167 --> 00:04:22.367
-And got away with it.

00:04:22.367 --> 00:04:23.767
Not bad
-A little bit.

00:04:23.767 --> 00:04:26.033
This is -- Was this writing
for "The Ben Stiller Show"?

00:04:26.033 --> 00:04:27.200
-Yes, that's
"The Ben Stiller Show."

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:28.701
-So, you got Ben Stiller.

00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:30.634
-And now you know
why I have a beard.

00:04:30.634 --> 00:04:34.000
Because I look weird.
-Yeah. There you go.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:35.334
-This is...
I mean, this is...

00:04:35.334 --> 00:04:37.133
Look at this A-list crew
right here.

00:04:37.133 --> 00:04:39.400
So, and this is on the set
of "The Larry Sanders Show."

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:41.400
-It's "The Larry Sanders Show."
And so that's with Gary,

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:43.968
and that's Warren Beatty,
and Rip Torn, and me, young.

00:04:43.968 --> 00:04:45.467
But here's the thing.

00:04:45.467 --> 00:04:50.133
You know, I am them there.
Right?

00:04:50.133 --> 00:04:52.901
Like, so that's like
the Please Don't Destroy guys,

00:04:52.901 --> 00:04:54.334
and I'm Rip Torn now.
-Yeah.

00:04:54.334 --> 00:04:55.701
-Right?

00:04:55.701 --> 00:04:57.501
-But you liked,
and I know from...

00:04:57.501 --> 00:04:59.133
Because you were always,
as a kid,

00:04:59.133 --> 00:05:01.067
fascinated with comedy.
You went out of your way

00:05:01.067 --> 00:05:02.734
as a kid
to interview older comedians.

00:05:02.734 --> 00:05:04.133
-Yes.
-So it must be weird.

00:05:04.133 --> 00:05:05.968
I know you have a lot of respect
for younger comedians,

00:05:05.968 --> 00:05:07.033
but are you still out there

00:05:07.033 --> 00:05:08.467
trying to talk
to older comedians?

00:05:08.467 --> 00:05:10.734
-Well, now I have
to make older friends

00:05:10.734 --> 00:05:13.567
so I can still be the young guy.
-Yeah.

00:05:13.567 --> 00:05:15.434
-That's my new thing.
-Wait, let me see.

00:05:15.434 --> 00:05:17.567
Because I want to make sure
we have one. This is...

00:05:17.567 --> 00:05:19.534
-So, now, in order
to be the young guy,

00:05:19.534 --> 00:05:21.033
I have to hang out
with Mel Brooks.

00:05:21.033 --> 00:05:22.100
-Yeah.

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:24.734
-And Dick Van Dyke
and Norman Lear.

00:05:24.734 --> 00:05:26.734
That's 300 years of comedy
right there.

00:05:26.734 --> 00:05:29.300
-They're literally
300 years old.

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:33.234
That's the only way you can
feel like John Higgins today

00:05:33.234 --> 00:05:35.334
is to hang out with
300-year-old men.

00:05:35.334 --> 00:05:36.667
-Yeah, and talk about...

00:05:36.667 --> 00:05:38.834
And talk about World War II
for hours and hours.

00:05:38.834 --> 00:05:42.000
-I bet you talk about boners
a little bit.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:44.501
-But they're still
all way funnier than us.

00:05:44.501 --> 00:05:45.801
-Yes.
-Like, not even close.

00:05:45.801 --> 00:05:47.868
-It's really amazing.
I mean, it does...

00:05:47.868 --> 00:05:50.567
You know, I've been lucky enough
to meet a couple of them,

00:05:50.567 --> 00:05:53.534
and it's crazy how good they
still are at telling stories.

00:05:53.534 --> 00:05:56.634
-Oh, yeah. Me and Bill Hader
went to visit Mel Brooks once,

00:05:56.634 --> 00:05:59.133
and when we left, he said,
"Come, come visit again.

00:05:59.133 --> 00:06:01.033
But not soon."

00:06:01.033 --> 00:06:02.701
[ Laughter ]

00:06:02.701 --> 00:06:04.968
"Like four months.
Wait four months."

00:06:04.968 --> 00:06:06.467
[ Laughter ]

00:06:06.467 --> 00:06:08.934
And this was my group of, like,
Please Don't Destroy.

00:06:08.934 --> 00:06:12.934
This is us when we were young.
-And you've been opening

00:06:12.934 --> 00:06:15.000
for Adam Sandler recently
on the road.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:17.767
He's doing a standup tour,
and you've been opening for him

00:06:17.767 --> 00:06:19.234
for 40 years.
Is that true?

00:06:19.234 --> 00:06:21.100
-Yeah, I mean, I literally
started doing standup...

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:23.434
It'll be -- In a year
and a half, it'll be 40 years.

00:06:23.434 --> 00:06:25.601
-Wow.
-Since the first time.

00:06:25.601 --> 00:06:27.868
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:27.868 --> 00:06:30.567
I always think that anything
that's like 40 years earlier...

00:06:30.567 --> 00:06:32.434
I always think like,
if this was the '70s,

00:06:32.434 --> 00:06:34.267
I'd be talking
about W.C. Fields.

00:06:34.267 --> 00:06:36.234
-Yeah.
-Like, it's too much.

00:06:36.234 --> 00:06:37.734
And the sad part is,
it's been 40 years

00:06:37.734 --> 00:06:40.400
and I'm still opening for Adam.
You know?

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:42.334
It never, like, reversed.
-It never flipped.

00:06:42.334 --> 00:06:44.467
You never got anybody
who would open for you.

00:06:44.467 --> 00:06:45.968
-No, that's not possible.

00:06:45.968 --> 00:06:48.133
But I lately have been thinking
I should stop doing standup,

00:06:48.133 --> 00:06:49.400
and I've been, like,
kind of making a list,

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:52.234
like pros and cons of,
like, should I quit?

00:06:52.234 --> 00:06:53.601
-Oh, that's interesting.

00:06:53.601 --> 00:06:56.033
And so these are the reasons
why I should quit.

00:06:56.033 --> 00:06:59.367
-Should?
-Yes.

00:06:59.367 --> 00:07:03.634
"Too bad a memory to remember
jokes; must read off paper."

00:07:03.634 --> 00:07:05.300
-Yeah.

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:06.801
-"Nothing to say."

00:07:06.801 --> 00:07:08.067
-Nothing to say.

00:07:08.067 --> 00:07:10.267
-"Too rich to bother
writing jokes."

00:07:10.267 --> 00:07:12.767
-Yeah.
That'll get in the way.

00:07:12.767 --> 00:07:15.601
-"Don't know who Doja Cat is."
-Yeah.

00:07:15.601 --> 00:07:17.400
Who -- Who does?

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:19.901
-"No endgame."
-[ Laughs ]

00:07:19.901 --> 00:07:23.300
-"Hard to do with sciatica."
-Yeah.

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:26.300
-"All my references
are from the 1900s."

00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:27.634
-Yeah.

00:07:27.634 --> 00:07:30.567
-"The verdict is in --
I'm not charismatic."

00:07:30.567 --> 00:07:33.400
-Oh, well, I would wait that out
a little bit, yeah.

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:34.567
-The last one is,

00:07:34.567 --> 00:07:36.834
"Sick of riding in buses
with Rob Schneider."

00:07:36.834 --> 00:07:37.834
-Yeah,

00:07:37.834 --> 00:07:39.200
-Which is actually
kind of fun.

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:40.868
-This is a crazy photo,

00:07:40.868 --> 00:07:44.100
because this is
the 1992 "Young Comedians."

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:46.434
-That's right.
-And I mean, I...

00:07:46.434 --> 00:07:49.367
Everybody here had a career.

00:07:49.367 --> 00:07:51.367
It's a really impressive
group of people.

00:07:51.367 --> 00:07:53.467
-That's right. Yeah.
And look at Carvey.

00:07:53.467 --> 00:07:55.934
Carvey looks the same.
-Yeah.

00:07:55.934 --> 00:07:57.601
-Look at me.
-Yeah.

00:07:57.601 --> 00:07:58.934
-The difference.

00:07:58.934 --> 00:08:02.067
-It is haunting
how much different you look

00:08:02.067 --> 00:08:04.400
and how much the same he looks.
-Yeah.

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:06.701
This is the thing.
Here's my other list.

00:08:06.701 --> 00:08:08.167
Ready?
-You have two lists.

00:08:08.167 --> 00:08:10.200
-My second list, which I can't
remember, which is, you know,

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:11.801
you have to stop doing standup
at a certain age

00:08:11.801 --> 00:08:14.033
'cause all your jokes
become about decay.

00:08:14.033 --> 00:08:15.567
-Yeah.
-Right?

00:08:15.567 --> 00:08:19.167
Because you -- that's all
you know in life, is decay.

00:08:19.167 --> 00:08:21.534
And so all your jokes
are kind of like,

00:08:21.534 --> 00:08:23.667
"Hey, isn't diverticulitis
funny?"

00:08:23.667 --> 00:08:25.334
-Yeah.

00:08:25.334 --> 00:08:26.501
-"Don't you hate it

00:08:26.501 --> 00:08:28.000
when all your girlfriends
from high school

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:30.167
start dying of natural causes?"

00:08:30.167 --> 00:08:31.968
[ Laughter ]

00:08:31.968 --> 00:08:34.734
-That's a good one.
I like that one.

00:08:34.734 --> 00:08:36.234
I mean, I don't know
if it's a closer,

00:08:36.234 --> 00:08:38.234
'cause everybody's
super bummed out, but...

00:08:38.234 --> 00:08:39.601
[ Both laugh ]
-It's both.

00:08:39.601 --> 00:08:41.033
-It is a delight
to have you here.

00:08:41.033 --> 00:08:43.501
Congrats on the movie.
That's Judd Apatow, everybody.

00:08:43.501 --> 00:08:45.734
-"Please Don't Destroy:
The Treasure of Foggy Mountain"

00:08:45.734 --> 00:08:48.400
begins streaming exclusively
on Peacock November 17th.

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:50.467
We'll be right back with music
from Rett Madison.

